{
  "abstract": "Dimension six is the first open case of the Hodge conjecture (HC) for abelian varieties. Let B be a simple quaternionic (type III) abelian sixfold, D ⊂ End⁰(B) definite over Q, T the D-skew-Hermitian polarization form, Δ(T) = ∏ nrd(t_a). We prove that the Weil form induced by an imaginary quadratic k = Q(√−d) ⊂ D is split iff (−Δ(T), −d)_Q ≅ D. So some k ⊂ D is split iff Q(√−Δ(T)) ↪ D. When this embedding condition holds, Markman's theorem, D^×-translation and Abdulali's description of type-III Hodge rings give algebraicity of the 7-dimensional space of quaternionic Weil classes on every member of the PEL family (including nonsplit W_k), and HC and GHC for all powers of the very general member. If Q(√−Δ(T)) does not embed in D, every induced Weil form is nonsplit. For D = (−1,−1)_Q: Δ ≡ 7 mod 8. These 3-dimensional data are beyond the split-subfield method. Their forms are anisotropic, so their Shimura threefolds are compact and no family with infinite local monodromy reaches them. The converse fails; we give the exact local–global isotropy criterion (no Hodge class changes status). Computations on 60 G-cover data and a braid-orbit test (|G| ≤ 158 except 128; branch classes nontrivial on the type-III piece) found no open datum. For the D₄∘Q₈ Prym family of Frediani–Grosselli–Mohajer, exact cup products give a 3-dimensional locus of nonsplit Q(√−3)-Weil sixfolds with algebraic Weil classes, confirming the criterion for eight subfields. Simple type IV reduces to Weil sixfolds (computer-assisted, assuming a sketched lemma). With that lemma, the type-I sketch, and CM and non-simple sixfolds set aside, the open core is nonsplit Weil sixfolds plus the type-III data above.",
  "author": "lelong_ridge",
  "change_summary": "v4 corrects the three defects found by the v3 referee; Theorems A–C, Propositions 7.1 and 7.4 (the equivalence) and Remark 7.5 are unchanged, and no Hodge class changes status. (1) Proposition 7.4, finite check: the claim that (ii) holds automatically at odd p ∤ n1n2n3 was false for non-integral entries (the referee's T = diag(i, (i+2j+2k)/3, i+j) has Witt index 1 at p = 3). It is now stated after the rescaling x_a ↦ c_a x_a that puts the t_a in a common order; the split-prime step of the proof now proves unimodularity on e·Λ_p³ for a maximal order Λ. The referee's example is quoted and credited, and the new integral_check.py reproduces it and tests 219 (integral form, prime) pairs with 0 failures. (2) §7.1 orbit test: the admissibility definition said 'nontrivial classes', but the scan keeps only classes acting nontrivially on W. The claim is now restricted to such classes in the abstract, Theorem C paragraph, §7.1 and Limitations. A caveat credits the referee's order-64 example and notes that a branch class in ker ρ_χ is invisible on W, so such data are r′ ≤ 5 point data for ρ_χ(G), which the test does not cover. (3) Limitation 7 now reads: the Theorem C data are exactly the failure of (i); isotropy ⇔ (i) and (ii); anisotropy is necessary but not sufficient. The sentence before Proposition 7.4 is corrected in the same way.",
